---
import Footer from "@/components/site/footer.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/base.astro";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "what-is-kaji", text: "What is Kaji?" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "how-it-works", text: "How it works" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "what-you-provide", text: "What you provide" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Introduction"
  description="Kaji runs application actions safely when an agent, automation, or API caller asks for them."
  activeHref="/introduction"
  headings={headings}
>
  <article class="article doc-article">
    <header>
      <h1>Introduction</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        Kaji runs application actions, like refunding a payment, safely when something other than a
        person clicking a button asks for them.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 id="what-is-kaji">What is Kaji?</h2>
      <p>
        Your app already has functions such as <code>refundPayment()</code>, <code
          >cancelBooking()</code
        >, and <code>sendEmail()</code>. When an agent, automation, or API caller can trigger them,
        you need to check the input, check the caller is allowed, avoid running the same request
        twice, and handle failures where you cannot tell what happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kaji does these checks in one place. It is a TypeScript library with no dependencies, and it
        runs inside your app.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Define a capability: the input, the permission check, and the function that does the work.</li>
        <li>Create a Kaji instance with a store.</li>
        <li>
          Call <code>kaji.execute()</code> with the input, a <code>principalId</code>, and an
          <code>idempotencyKey</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Read the <code>status</code> of the result: <code>succeeded</code>, <code>denied</code>,
          <code>rejected</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>cancelled</code>, or
          <code>unknown</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="what-you-provide">What you provide</h2>
      <p>
        Kaji does not log users in, decide who may do what, build approval screens, retry failed
        work, or save data for production. You provide the user identity, the permission rules, the
        approval step, and a store. Kaji calls them in the right order.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="quickstart-links">
      <p>
        Continue with <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a> to define and run a
        capability.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
